cite176†developer contributions†github.com .\nL326: \nL327: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite177†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL328: cite178†About†info.arxiv.org · cite179†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite180†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite181†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite182†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite183†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite184†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite185†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L329: \nL330: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKREX: Concurrent Kernel Benchmarking on Shared GPUs via Region-Granular Exclusivity (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.30057v1)\nciteturn38view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.30057v1\",\"pattern\":\"Affiliation\"}); Total lines: 331\nL38: cite28†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L39: \nL40: arXiv:2609.30057v1 [cs.DC] 24 Sep 2026\nL41: # KREX: Concurrent Kernel Benchmarking on Shared GPUs via Region-Granular Exclusivity\nL42: Tianyu Feng^{*} Affiliation: HKUST, Hong Kong SAR, China , Haoxuan Yu^{*} Affiliation: HKUST, Hong Kong SAR, China , Tianyuan Wu Affiliation: HKUST, Hong Kong SAR, China , Lingyun Yang Affiliation: Alibaba Group, China , Daocheng Ying Affiliation: Alibaba Group, China , Yuxiao Wang Affiliation: Alibaba Group, China , Ruibo Fan Affiliation: Alibaba Group, China , Yinghao Yu Affiliation: Alibaba Group, China , Guodong Yang Affiliation: Alibaba Group, China , Liping Zhang Affiliation: Alibaba Group, China and Wei Wang Affiliation: HKUST, Hong Kong SAR, China\nL43: ###### Abstract.\nL44: LLM agents automate GPU kernel optimization by repeatedly composing candidates and measuring their duration on real GPUs. Existing systems preserve measurement fidelity by reserving a GPU for an entire agent session or benchmarking command. However, this results in poor utilization because only a small fraction of command execution requires exclusive GPU access. Sharing GPUs could recover this idle capacity, but introduces contention that compromises measurement fidelity and misdirects the agent’s search.\nL45: We present KREX, a runtime for concurrent kernel agent benchmarking with region-granular exclusivity. KREX lets agents mark critical regions involving timing-sensitive operations within a benchmarking command. The runtime then enforces exclusivity within marked regions and allows concurrent execution outside them, achieving high throughput while preserving measurement fidelity.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nXtrace: High-Fidelity GPU Intra-Kernel Tracing via Binary-Level Instruction Splicing (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.28769v1)\nciteturn38view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.28769v1\",\"pattern\":\"5.2\"}); Total lines: 541\nL29:     1. cite19†5.1 Experimental Setup L30:     2. cite20†5.2 Xtrace-Driven Agentic Kernel Optimization L31:       1. cite21†5.2.1 Case 1: Optimizing FA-3 across Tracing Tools L32:       2. cite22†5.2.2 Case 2: Optimizing FA-4 against cuDNN Trace L33:     3. cite23†5.3 Binary Similarity L34:     4. cite24†5.4 Tracing Overhead L35:       1. cite25†5.4.1 Metrics L36:       2. cite26†5.4.2 End-to-end Duration L37:       3. cite27†5.4.3 Dependency Waits L38:     5. cite28†5.5 Reusing Dead-Period Registers L39:     6. cite29†5.6 Splicing Time L40:   7. cite30†6 Conclusion L41:   8. cite31†References L42:   9. cite32†A Appendix L43:     1. cite33†A.1 Trace Result of Closed-Sourced Vendor Kernels. L44:     2. cite34†A.2 Per-Vendor Splicing Details. L45:       1. cite35†A.2.1 NVIDIA GPUs. L46:       2. cite36†A.2.2 AMD GPUs. L47: cite37†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L48: \nL49: arXiv:2609.28769v1 [cs.AR] 23 Sep 2026\nL59: Xtrace is the first GPU kernel tracing system with near-zero compile-time interference and minimized runtime overhead. Xtrace inserts probes directly into the compiled kernel binary. It reuses only the registers that hold dead values at the insertion address and resolves all hazards with the compiler’s hazard tables. It further schedules the instruction order, register allocation, and control bits to minimize the runtime overhead the probe introduces.\nL60: Xtrace supports 19 NVIDIA and AMD GPU architectures, and is publicly available for use at cite39†https://g-watch.github.io†g-watch.github.io .\nL61: We evaluate Xtrace on major production large language model (LLM) kernels against the state-of-the-art tracers Neutrino and IKET from NVIDIA. On H100, B300, and MI300X GPUs, Xtrace preserves 94–98% of the instructions of the kernel, while existing tools preserve only 8–48%. Xtrace adds only 0.9–2.8% overhead, while existing tools add 3.8–75.6%. Xtrace guides a coding agent to reach the same FlashAttention-3 performance with 3.9$\\times$ fewer iterations than existing traces do.\nL62: Thanks to our binary-level instrumentation, Xtrace also traces the faster closed-source cuDNN kernel, which guides the agent to lift the open-source FlashAttention-4 by 5.2–13.3% in throughput.\nL63: ## 1. Introduction\nL64: \nL65: (a) Binary similarity.\nL66: \nL67: (b) Tracing overhead.\nL68: \nL69: Figure 1. Two sources of the tracing fidelity loss in Neutrino [cite40†36 ] and IKET [cite41†57 ] across three representative kernels.\nL70: Global investment in AI infrastructure continues to grow rapidly [cite42†29 , cite43†38 ], with GPUs forming a major part of the cost of training and serving large language models (LLMs) [cite44†19 , cite45†60 ]. To improve GPU utilization, modern LLM workloads increasingly rely on fused kernels [cite46†22 , cite47†64 , cite48†74 , cite49†26 , cite50†28 , cite51†25 , cite52†24 , cite53†77 , cite54†3 ], which combine computation, memory accesses, and communication within a single kernel.\nL71: Optimizing such kernels requires accurate timing information to expose instruction overlap, pipeline bubbles, and idle periods [cite55†35 , cite47†64 , cite56†63 , cite57†62 , cite58†16 ]. However, traditional GPU profilers mainly report aggregate statistics from hardware counters [cite59†61 , cite60†56 , cite61†12 ], which are insufficient to reveal these fine-grained timing relationships\nL97: We show that high-fidelity tracing significantly benefits agentic kernel optimization. Xtrace guides an agent to reach the same FA-3 performance in 3.9$\\times$ fewer iterations than the best existing tracing tool, and enables trace-guided optimization of FA-4 against closed-source cuDNN, lifting its throughput by 5.2–13.3%.\nL98: ## 2. Background & Motivation\nL99: ### 2.1. GPU Intra-Kernel Tracing\nL100: High-performance GPU kernels [cite51†25 , cite79†71 , cite80†32 , cite81†20 ] tend to fuse more work into a single kernel launch to saturate GPU utilization and cut CPU-side launch overhead. These kernels such as FA-3 [cite47†64 ] build complex software pipelines to exploit both compute and memory bandwidth. Therefore, their monolithic execution becomes harder to profile with traditional counter-based profilers [cite59†61 , cite60†56 , cite61†12 ].\nL240: ## 5. Evaluation\nL241: \nL242: The evaluation answers the following questions:\nL243: RQ1. Does Xtrace’s higher tracing fidelity lead to more effective optimizations? (§cite20†5.2 )\nL244: RQ2. Does Xtrace keep the kernel binary unchanged? (§cite23†5.3 )\nL245: RQ3. How much overhead does Xtrace add, and what does each mechanism save? (§cite24†5.4 –§cite29†5.6 )\nL246: ### 5.1. Experimental Setup\nL247: \nL248: GPU architectures. Xtrace supports 19 GPU architectures across NVIDIA and AMD, as Table cite119†2 lists. The support spans from Volta to Blackwell on NVIDIA and covers both the CDNA and RDNA product lines on AMD. Note that the disassembler (§cite11†3 ) covers the full instruction set of each architecture, since the splicing analysis must decode every instruction correctly. Due to space limitations, we defer the vendor-specific GPU implementation details to Appendix cite32†A .\nL255: trace-pp splices probes before and after each pipeline stage of the kernel, recording a timestamp and region index at each stage boundary, as in §cite9†2.2 . trace-mem splices a probe at every memory access, recording the 32-bit shared-memory or 64-bit device-memory address and a timestamp. These trace types are ordered from light to heavy by their recording workload.\nL256: Every probe records once per warp, from an elected lane: one of the tools records per warp by construction (§cite19†5.1 ), so a per-thread record would compare tools at different recording workloads.\nL257: Baselines. Neutrino [cite40†36 ] represents IR-level tracing and covers both vendors. IKET [cite41†57 ] represents binary-level instruction replacement but supports only CuTeDSL kernels on NVIDIA GPUs. IKET inserts a placeholder at the source level and replaces it at the binary level. We reimplement this mechanism inside Xtrace and extend it to every workload. We do not extend it to AMD GPUs, as their toolchain provides no equivalent placeholder mechanism.\nL258: We refer to both the original tool and this reimplementation as IKET. The unprobed kernel serves as the ground truth for binary similarity and as the reference for overhead. Binary-level tools such as NVBit [cite76†69 ] pay a structurally larger overhead, and thus are excluded (§cite10†2.3 ). We run Xtrace with the optimal splicing by default, and Xtrace-g replaces the optimal search with the greedy order.\nL259: Figure 11. (Case 1) Agentic optimization of FA-3 on H100 using Xtrace, Neutrino and IKET.\nL260: ### 5.2. Xtrace-Driven Agentic Kernel Optimization\nL261: First, to examine whether trace fidelity translates into performance gains, we task an AI code agent with optimizing FA-3 with the traces from Xtrace, Neutrino and IKET, echoing the measurement of §cite9†2.2 . Second, to leverage the binary-level tracing only Xtrace provides, we run the agent on the open-source FA-4 with the trace of cuDNN SDPA, the vendor’s closed-source optimized version of FA-4 [cite127†75 ].\nL262: This case examines whether the agent can recover the vendor’s optimizations from the trace alone and apply them to FA-4.\nL263: cite132†Image: Refer to caption Figure 12. Binary corruption under probing, as the preserved basic blocks (a) and original instructions (b).\nL264: #### 5.2.1. Case 1: Optimizing FA-3 across Tracing Tools\nL265: We task a coding agent with optimizing FA-3 on an H100 GPU for higher TFLOPS, the same kernel as in §cite9†2.2 . We run four sessions on the same agent harness that differ only in the profile fed to the agent, either counter-based profiling alone or one of the three traces. The three traces are trace-pp runs of the first iteration, so they all describe the same kernel.\nL266: Guided by the Xtrace trace, the agent locks the true bottleneck: the producer warp waits 1,298 ns of a 1,435 ns window for the consumer warps to release the K tile buffer (Figure cite133†11 b). The Neutrino and IKET traces instead split the wait between the K and V tiles, reporting an inflated V-tile wait of 1,024–1,216 ns, as their probes defer the release of the V tile past the SoftMax (§cite9†2.2 ).\nL267: Every session ends at about the same 566 TFLOPS, but they reach that level at very different iterations (Figure cite133†11 a). Benefiting from precise localization of the true bottleneck, the Xtrace-guided agent gets there at the 14th iteration, against the 54th with Neutrino and the 71st with IKET, while the agent without a trace needs 85. Xtrace thus reaches the same performance 3.9$\\times$ sooner than the Neutrino trace, the fastest of the three baselines.\nL268: #### 5.2.2. Case 2: Optimizing FA-4 against cuDNN Trace\nL269: \nL270: Since the FA-4 code release, the closed-source cuDNN SDPA has absorbed its design and further overtaken its performance [cite127†75 ]. We trace both with Xtrace and ask the agent to optimize FA-4 from the differences between the two traces.\nL271: Finding 1: FA-4 retires its CTAs in waves while cuDNN stays resident. First, the traces differ in how many CTAs each kernel keeps resident. As shown in Figure cite134†10 b, FA-4 launches one CTA per work tile, so its 512 CTAs retire in four waves over 148 SMs. The tail wave is dispatched only after 84% of the kernel has elapsed, and every SM idles at each wave boundary. As shown in Figure cite134†10 a, cuDNN keeps a single CTA resident on every SM for the whole kernel.\nL272: Therefore, the agent makes the FA-4 grid persistent with Cluster Launch Control (CLC) [cite135†59 ], which hands a CTA its next work tile in place, as illustrated in Figure cite134†10 c. Since CLC returns raw grid coordinates, the agent also keeps the grouping that holds concurrently resident tiles on the same keys and values. The change lifts throughput by 9.4%.\nL273: Finding 2: The persistent grid leaves CTAs unevenly loaded. Second, the trace shows that the persistent grid moves the bottleneck onto load balance. Every CTA now carries one SM’s entire share of the work, so the longest-resident CTA sets the kernel duration. The traced residencies differ by 1.49$\\times$ between the longest and the shortest CTA, because FA-4 orders its tiles from the longest to the shortest only within one group, and the heaviest tile of every group is therefore issued at the same time.\nL274: The agent therefore orders the tiles across all the groups instead of within each one, so that the heaviest tiles are spread over the CTAs rather than issued together. As shown in Figure cite134†10 c, this narrows the residency difference to 1.18$\\times$ and lifts throughput by another 2.0%.\nL275: cite136†Image: Refer to caption Figure 13. Probe overhead per trace type, as the normalized execution time (a) and the dependency waits the probes themselves incur, as a share of the unprobed kernel’s (b).\nL276: Finding 3: CLC pairs long tiles with long ones. Third, on the shapes where a CTA takes at most two tiles, the traces differ in which tiles share a CTA. Under a causal mask the work of a tile grows with its query block index, so a balanced schedule pairs a long tile with a short one on the same CTA. The durations of the two tiles on a cuDNN CTA correlate at $-0.81$, while those on an FA-4 CTA correlate at $+0.21$, as CLC resolves the dispatch order in hardware.\nL277: The agent therefore falls back to a static schedule on these shapes, fixing the tiles each CTA takes. As shown in Figure cite134†10 d, the correlation moves to $-0.76$ and throughput rises by 11.1%.\nL278: Finding 4: The epilogue waits on a last tile that nothing follows on both FA-4 and cuDNN. Finally, the epilogue warp’s trace shows what writing out a work tile costs. After writing out a tile, the FA-4 epilogue waits for the write to complete and then releases the buffer for the next tile to reuse. However, as shown in Figure cite134†10 b, the FA-4 epilogue still waits on the last tile even though nothing follows it, wasting 544 ns per CTA.\nL279: We find that cuDNN also unnecessarily waits on its last tile, as shown in Figure cite134†10 a. The agent therefore drops the wait on the last tile, as shown in Figure cite134†10 c, which lifts throughput by another 1.2%.\nL280: Together the four changes lift FA-4 on B300 by 5.2% to 13.3% in throughput. The optimized kernel matches cuDNN on three of the four shapes and runs 5.2% faster on the fourth (Figure cite134†10 d). We release more case studies on closed-source kernels at cite137†https://g-watch.github.io/en/open-traces/†g-watch.github.io .\nL281: ### 5.3. Binary Similarity\nL282: Metrics. We evaluate how much of the compiled binary each tracing tool preserves under different tracing workloads. We quantify the preservation by counting the basic blocks that change in the probed kernel’s Control-Flow Graph (CFG), and the instructions that differ from the unprobed kernel within the traced region. We align the instruction sequences of the probed and the unprobed kernel.\nL283: A basic block counts as changed when any of its original instructions is altered, dropped, or moved into another block; the inserted probe instructions are not counted against it. An instruction counts as changed when its operands, its control bits, or its position within its basic block differ.\nL284: Similarity across trace types. As shown in Figure cite138†12 , Xtrace changes no basic block and preserves 94–98% of the original instructions under every trace type, as splicing only adds the probe instructions. The changed 2–6% are branch instructions whose target addresses must shift after inserting instructions. Neutrino changes 61–82% of the basic blocks and rewrites 54–92% of the instructions, and IKET changes 8–80% of the blocks and rewrites 5–87% of the instructions.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn38view5 [wordlim: 200] Unable to resolve click call due to invalid arguments\n"
